        "ores tiny wobbles (a fraction of a degree). Our Moon is turned by the IAU’s model of its rotation (from 1800 to 2100): its axis leans about 1.5° and it rocks slowly (libration), so from Earth a little more than half of it can be seen over time; the point under Earth matches JPL’s to within 0.01°. Outside those years its near side simply faces Earth, its axis upright. Eight other moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto, Rhea, Triton, Charon and Phobos) wear black-and-white maps from USGS (Galileo, Voyager, Cassini, New Horizons and Viking pictures; the parts never photographed are plain grey) and turn by the IAU’s rotation models; the rest (Titan, whose haze hides its surface, Enceladus, Titania, Oberon and Deimos) are plain grey balls: their colours are not measured here. The Sun does not get a real spin yet.</p>"},
    {"trip", "🚀 Journey and jumping to a date",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>🚀 Journey</b> (in the Journey and dates card) zooms out by itself: Earth, the Moon’s orbit, Earth’s orbit, all the planets, Voyager 1, the Oort cloud, the nearest stars, the Pleiades and Orion Nebula, and the centre of our galaxy, with a caption at each stop. It runs at 1 second = 1 hour so you can see Earth turn, and your own Time speed comes back at the end. Touch the picture, the zoom or any tile to stop it.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Jump to a date</b> puts every planet, dwarf planet, comet and belt where it really was or will be on any date from 1800 to 2100 (Greenwich time), using NASA/JPL’s orbit numbers, which are made for 1800–2050 (after 2050 the places are extended and get slowly less exact). Try your birthday. Check: on 27 Aug 2003 Mars is 55.8 million km from Earth, as NASA says, and on 21 Dec 2020 Jupiter and Saturn are 0.1° apart.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Not real on a new date:</b> any moon before 1800 or after 2100 (they start at made-up places, and the Moon falls back to a simple ellipse; from 1800 to 2100 the Moon follows ELP2000-82B in Simple too, and the other 13 big moons follow orbits fitted to JPL Horizons, within 15 km for Charon to 17,700 km for Europa), and the Voyagers, which only show from 1990 when both were flying in straight lines.</p>"},
    {"time", "⏱ What does Time speed mean?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">The Time speed slider decides how fast the lab’s time runs. It is set to <b>{TSPEED}</b> right now: that many days (or seconds) of lab time go by for every real second you watch.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">The planets need very different times for one lap: Mercury about 88 days, Earth one year, Neptune about 165 years. So a speed that shows the outer planets moving is far too fast for Mercury, and a speed that lets you watch a moon is far too slow for Neptune. The date in the top bar counts the lab’s time. <b>☉ Reset to today</b> puts everything back where it is today.</p>"},
    {"scale", "📏 What is to scale, and what is not",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the distances, and the planets’ orbits. With <b>Easy to see</b> on, the planets are drawn bigger than they really are, and the Sun about <b>32 times</b> too big, so you can find them. Their distances are still to scale.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours of the orbits and dots, the tails of the comets (their real length changes, and they only grow near the Sun), and the moons’ orbits. Moons are so close to their planet that in a true picture they would hide inside it, so their orbits are drawn much wider than real until you zoom far in. The card of the moon says how many times too wide.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Only the largest:</b> the belts are drawn with 3,000 real asteroids, the largest known of each kind (JPL’s Small-Body Database): 1,800 in the asteroid belt, 300 Trojans and 900 beyond Neptune, each on the orbit of its record (Jupiter’s pull on them is not modelled, so far from 2026 they drift from their real places). In reality the asteroid belt has about 1.2 million rocks wider than 1 km, give or take 0.5 million (Tedesco and Désert 2002). The Oort cloud’s dots are made up: it is thought to hold trillions of icy bodies that nobody has seen directly.</p>\n      {PHYSICS}"},
    {"bary", "⚖️ Why does the Sun wobble?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">The planets do not really orbit the <i>centre of the Sun</i>. The Sun and everything around it circle their common balance point, the <b>barycentre</b>. Because the Sun is so heavy, that point is always very close to it, but it is not exactly at its centre.</p>\n      <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Right now the balance point is about <b>{BARY} Sun radii</b> from the Sun’s centre.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Over the years it wanders from inside the Sun to about 2 Sun radii out. Most of the pull comes from Jupiter, the heaviest planet. The whole picture is drawn around the barycentre, so the Sun is the one that wobbles. Pick <b>Barycentre</b> in Controls › Pick a body to look at it.</div></div>"},
    {"comet", "☄️ What is a comet, and why the tails?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">A comet is a ball of ice and dust, usually only a few kilometres across. Its orbit is a long, stretched ellipse. Far from the Sun most comets are frozen and dark, with no tail (the lab draws none beyond 4 AU, a drawing rule: real comets can be active much farther out).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Near the Sun, the ice turns to gas and blows off, and the comet grows a glowing cloud and <b>two tails</b>: a thin, straight <b>blue gas tail</b> and a wider, curved <b>yellow dust tail</b>. The thin gas tail points straight <b>away from the Sun</b>, no matter which way the comet is moving (the solar wind pushes it). The dust tail is pushed by sunlight more gently, so it lags behind the comet and curves along its orbit; seen from Earth it can even look like a spike pointing toward the Sun. On the way out a comet flies gas tail first.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Halley’s Comet goes round the Sun backwards compared with the planets, and is back about every 76 years (next in 2061).</p>"},
    {"move", "🧭 How do I move around?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Zoom</b> with the scroll wheel, a pinch, or the Zoom slider: zooming flies the camera closer (in 3D the view has a 45-degree field, like a phone camera, and the stars behind are drawn through the same lens, in the direction the camera looks: they are too far away for its place to matter). <b>Move the view</b> by dragging. <b>Tap</b> anything for its facts card. <b>Controls › Pick a body</b> flies you to a planet, moon, comet or belt and keeps your Time speed as you set it.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Lost? <b>↺ Reset view</b> brings back the whole picture.</p>"},
  },
  {
    {"big", "🔎 The big picture",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">This is a map of our <b>Solar System</b>: the Sun, eight planets, dwarf planets, moons, belts of rocks and comets. The planets, dwarf planets, comets and big moons (from 1800 to 2100) are drawn where they really are on the date shown in the middle of the top bar, and so are the belts’ rocks, which are real asteroids; the Oort cloud’s dots are not (see below).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">You are in the <b>2D view</b>: you look straight down from above, on the north side of the Solar System. Seen from there, every planet goes round <b>counter-clockwise</b>.</p>\n      <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">{WHEN}</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">You have selected: <b>{CARD}</b>. Its facts are in the card on the picture.</div></div>"},
    {"see", "👀 What is each thing on the picture?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Coloured balls with names</b> are the Sun, the planets, the dwarf planets and (when you zoom in) the moons. The faint line behind a planet is the path it has just travelled.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Grey circles with “AU”</b> are distance rings. <b>1 AU</b> is a fixed length, 149,597,870.7 km (the IAU defined it so in 2012); it is close to the Earth’s average distance from the Sun, about 150 million km. The ring marked 20 AU is 20 times as far from the Sun as we are. Light needs about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to cross 1 AU.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The faint green band</b> is the <b>habitable zone</b>: the range of distances where a planet’s surface could be warm enough for liquid water. For the Sun this lab uses 0.95 to 1.68 AU, the estimate of Kopparapu and others (2014) for a planet of Earth’s mass. It is a guide from a simple climate model, and scientists give different numbers.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Clouds of tiny dots</b> are the belts: the asteroid belt (between Mars and Jupiter), Jupiter’s Trojans, the Kuiper belt beyond Neptune, and far out the Oort cloud.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Dotted coloured lines</b> are the orbits of the six famous comets. A comet has a glowing head, and near the Sun two tails.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Small open circles</b> around a body mean “it is here, but too small to see at this zoom”. Zoom in and the ball grows out of the circle.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The bar at the bottom left</b> is the scale bar: how long a round distance is at this zoom, and how long light needs to cross it. <b>The small arrows at the bottom right</b> show the directions: x and y lie in t"
        "he flat plane of the orbits, z points straight up out of it.</p>"},
    {"sky", "🌌 The stars and the far-away things",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The stars in the background</b> (the <b>Soft</b> setting keeps them dim while you look at the planets and brightens them as you zoom out; <b>Full</b> keeps them bright) are real: the 2,874 brightest ones (about what your eyes see in a dark place), each in its true direction, drawn in the colour of a black body at the star’s colour temperature, worked out from its measured B−V colour (Ballesteros 2012; real star spectra differ a little). Brightness on screen follows each star’s measured magnitude through one rule: the light drawn grows as the square root of the star’s real light (5 magnitudes, a factor of 100 in light, become a factor of 10 on screen), so the faintest stars stay visible; a screen cannot show the real range. Behind them is a real picture of the whole sky: ESA’s Gaia map of the Milky Way, made from more than 1.8 billion stars, with its dark dust lanes and, in the far south, the two Magellanic Clouds. They are so far away that they do not move when you zoom or follow something. They only move when you turn the picture in the 3D view (in 2D you look straight down, so you see the half of the sky below the Solar System).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Constellations</b> (in the Sky card) draws the lines people drew between stars long ago. The stars of a constellation are usually not near each other: they only look that way from here.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Far-away things:</b> zoom out a very long way (or tap a tile under Far away). The <b>white rings with a coloured dot</b> are real stars at their real direction and distance, like Alpha Centauri, the nearest star system, 4.3 light years away. The distance rings count light years too. The <b>blue rings</b> are the Pleiades and the Orion Nebula, and the <b>ring with a black dot</b> is Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our galaxy, 27,000 light years away. The <b>yellow rings</b> are Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, the two spacecraft that have left the Sun’s wind. Tap any of them for facts.<"
        "/p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>What is to scale:</b> the directions and distances. The dots are markers, not sizes: at this scale even the Sun is less than a pixel. The Voyagers are at their real place on 30 Sep 2026 and then move along a straight line at their real speed, which is very close but not exact. Stars move slowly through space too, and that is ignored.</p>"},
    {"pink", "🎯 What is the pink ring?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>pink ring</b> means “look here”. It appears around whatever you selected, either by tapping it or from Controls › Pick a body. It has a dark edge so you can spot it on any background. Tap an empty spot on the picture to close the card and clear the selection.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Follow the selected body</b> (Controls › View options) keeps the selected thing in the middle while time runs. That is how you can watch a moon go round its planet, or stay with a comet as it swings past the Sun.</p>"},
    {"spin", "🌍 Do the planets really spin?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Yes, at their real speed and leaning the real way.</b> A dashed line through each planet is its <b>axis</b>, and <b>+</b> marks the end it turns counter-clockwise around (the right-hand rule, like Earth’s North Pole). For most planets that is the north pole; for Venus and Uranus, which turn backwards, it is the IAU’s south pole (the IAU calls the pole on the north side of the Solar System’s plane the north one). The directions and turning speeds come from the IAU (the body that names and measures the sky); for Saturn, Uranus and Neptune that is the radio period Voyager measured, and their cards also name the newer, better measurements.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Mercury, Earth, Mars, Jupiter and Pluto</b> wear real maps made from spacecraft pictures, turned the way each really is on the date shown in the top bar (Earth within about half a degree): Earth from NASA’s Blue Marble (January 2004, without clouds; NASA painted its deep oceans one even blue), seen through its air: the air scatters blue sunlight towards you (Rayleigh scattering, worked out for the real amount of air), so Earth looks bluer and hazier towards its edge, and the sunlight that reaches the ground near sunset is redder. Only light scattered once is worked out (light scattered again and again would add a little more blue), and the thin glow of air just beyond the edge is under a pixel. Mercury from MESSENGER (a black-and-white map, tinted so that its average colour is Mercury’s measured colour, from Mallama and others, 2017) and Pluto from New Horizons (in black and white: no colour map of all of Pluto could be found; its south was in winter darkness in 2015 and is plain grey), Mars from Viking, and Jupiter from Cassini (December 2000: its clouds move all the time, so its spots are not where they are today). <b>Saturn, Uranus and Neptune</b> wear Hubble’s maps from August and October 2025 (the OPAL programme), turned into the colours your eye would see from the light Hubble measured through each filter; famous pictures look more colourful because each filter is brightened on its own. The parts Hubble could not see (Saturn’s poles and the thin strip behind its rings, the half of Uranus and the north of Neptune that are in their long winter darkness) are filled with the colour next to them. <b>Venus</b> is wrapped in clouds with no markings to see, so it is a smooth ball in its real colour, worked out from how much light of each colour it reflects, measured through telescope filters (Mallama and others, 2017).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Light and shade:</b> each ball is lit from the real direction of the Sun, and the light is worked out in linear light (the picture colours are decoded from sRGB first), so the brightness falls off as the cosine of the Sun’s angle, as on a matte surface (Lambert’s law). Nothing lights the night side: it is the colour of the sky. Our <b>Moon</b> is not matte: at full Moon its edge is nearly as bright as its middle, so it follows McEwen’s lunar photometric function, fitted to Galileo pictures of the Moon (in the form used by Driver and others, 2025). The giant planets have uniform (Lambert) shading; real clouds darken towards the edge.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Brightness:</b> each map is scaled by one number so that the ball, seen fully lit, is as bright as the body measured (its geometric albedo in visible light: Mallama and others, 2017, for the planets; NASA&rsquo;s fact sheets for the Moon, Pluto and the moons). Limits: one albedo for the whole body; the ball is matte, so it has no extra brightening when fully lit (the real Moon and icy moons grow much brighter at full phase); a matte ball can be at most 2/3 as bright as a white disc, so Europa, Rhea and Triton (measured brighter than that) and Neptune are drawn darker than measured; a pixel pushed past white keeps its colour and is clipped (much of Jupiter&rsquo;s, Pluto&rsquo;s and Io&rsquo;s maps). <b>Earth&rsquo;s clouds</b> are NASA&rsquo;s cloud map of one moment (Blue Marble, 2002), not today&rsquo;s weather: flat, with no height and no shadows, lit by the Sun and seen through the air like the ground. Fully lit, Earth is drawn at about half its measured brightness (0.21 against 0.434).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Leans:</b> Earth 23.4°, Mars 25.2°, Saturn 26.7°, Neptune 28.3°, Jupiter only 3.1°. <b>Uranus</b> lies on its side (97.8°). <b>Venus</b> is upside down (177.4°) and turns backwards: one turn takes 243 days, longer than its 225-day year. Saturn’s rings lie in its equator, so they tilt with it. They are drawn from Cassini’s measurement: on 18 August 2008 the spacecraft watched the star beta Centauri shine through the rings and recorded how much of its light got through every 10 km, from the C ring out past the F ring (its optical depth, in ultraviolet light; the ring particles are far bigger than the light’s wavelength, so it is used for visible light too). So the Cassini Division, the Encke gap, the ringlets of the C ring and the nearly solid middle of the B ring are where they really are. It is one cut through the rings at one place: the rings’ finer clumps, waves and the F ring’s slightly oval shape are not in it, and each ring’s brightness still uses the middle of NASA’s measured range of albedo. How bright they look depends on where the Sun is: lit from your side the thick B ring shines; seen from the dark side it is black and the thin C ring and Cassini Division glow; when the Sun is in the ring plane (Saturn’s equinox, May 2025) they go almost dark. Saturn’s shadow falls on its rings, and the rings’ shadow on Saturn. <b>Eclipses</b> are where they really happen: the Moon’s shadow on Earth (try 8 April 2024, 18:17 UT), the black dots that Jupiter’s and Saturn’s moons cast on their planet, and a moon going dark in its planet’s shadow. The shadows are worked out from the places the lab gives the bodies on that date (the Moon’s within about 5 km of JPL’s), so they are where the real ones fall only as well as those places are known: the fitted orbits of Jupiter’s moons are off by up to 17,700 km for Europa (3,000 to 4,500 km for Io, Ganymede and Callisto), which can put a shadow or an eclipse about 20 minutes early or late for Europa and a few minutes for the others. The light’s travel time is left out (the aberration of sunlight, 20.5 arcseconds, moves the Moon’s shadow on Earth about 40 km, about a minute). The moons are drawn much farther from their planet than they are, but their shadows are not. In Earth’s shadow the Moon turns black here: the dim reddish light that Earth’s air bends into its shadow is left out. The Sun is taken as evenly bright to its edge, and Jupiter and Saturn cast round shadows (their slight flattening is left out); the grey moons without a map are not darkened. <b>Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune</b> have rings too (NASA’s ring fact sheets), drawn just as thin and dark as they are: Uranus’s narrow rings are darker than coal and show only as fine lines across the planet; Jupiter’s and Neptune’s are too faint to see in ordinary light. Left out: the very faint D, E and G rings of Saturn, Jupiter’s halo and gossamer rings, and the arcs of Neptune’s Adams ring; the beige colour of the rings is drawn, not measured: the same tint is used for the rings of Saturn, Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune (no table of their measured colours could be found), and Saturn’s fine ringlets are not drawn.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To watch one turn:</b> tap a planet, then <b>⏱ Slow time to watch it turn</b> (one turn takes about 24 seconds). At faster Time speeds a planet would turn more than once a second, so it is held still instead, and its card says so.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Each is drawn in its <b>real shape</b>: a turning planet bulges at its equator, so Saturn’s polar radius is 9.8 % smaller than its equatorial one and Jupiter’s 6.5 % (NASA fact sheets). <b>Simplified:</b> Earth’s turn ign"
        "ores tiny wobbles (a fraction of a degree). Our Moon is turned by the IAU’s model of its rotation (from 1800 to 2100): its axis leans about 1.5° and it rocks slowly (libration), so from Earth a little more than half of it can be seen over time; the point under Earth matches JPL’s to within 0.01°. Outside those years its near side simply faces Earth, its axis upright. Eight other moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto, Rhea, Triton, Charon and Phobos) wear black-and-white maps from USGS (Galileo, Voyager, Cassini, New Horizons and Viking pictures; the parts never photographed are plain grey) and turn by the IAU’s rotation models; the rest (Titan, whose haze hides its surface, Enceladus, Titania, Oberon and Deimos) are plain grey balls: their colours are not measured here. The Sun does not get a real spin yet.</p>"},
