    {"sky", "🌌 The stars and the far-away things",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The stars in the background</b> (the <b>Soft</b> setting keeps them dim while you look at the planets and brightens them as you zoom out; <b>Full</b> keeps them bright) are real: the 2,874 brightest ones (about what your eyes see in a dark place), each in its true direction, drawn in the colour of a black body at the star’s colour temperature, worked out from its measured B−V colour (Ballesteros 2012; real star spectra differ a little). Brightness on screen follows each star’s measured magnitude through one rule: the light drawn grows as the square root of the star’s real light (5 magnitudes, a factor of 100 in light, become a factor of 10 on screen), so the faintest stars stay visible; a screen cannot show the real range. Behind them is a real picture of the whole sky: ESA’s Gaia map of the Milky Way, made from more than 1.8 billion stars, with its dark dust lanes and, in the far south, the two Magellanic Clouds. They are so far away that they do not move when you zoom or follow something. They only move when you turn the picture.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Constellations</b> (in the Sky card) draws the lines people drew between stars long ago. The stars of a constellation are usually not near each other: they only look that way from here.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Far-away things:</b> zoom out a very long way (or tap a tile under Far away). The <b>white rings with a coloured dot</b> are real stars at their real direction and distance, like Alpha Centauri, the nearest star system, 4.3 light years away. The distance rings count light years too. The <b>blue rings</b> are the Pleiades and the Orion Nebula, and the <b>ring with a black dot</b> is Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our galaxy, 27,000 light years away. The <b>yellow rings</b> are Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, the two spacecraft that have left the Sun’s wind. Tap any of them for facts.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>What is to scale:</b> the directions and