static const char* const kAcenIntro = "The nearest stars to the Sun: what is measured, what is disputed, what was found to be wrong, and what nobody knows yet. The Orbit Lab draws it: Controls › System › Alpha Centauri.";
static const LearnSection kAcen[] = {
    {"stars", "🌟 Three stars",
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Alpha Centauri A</b> (a little bigger and brighter than the Sun) and <b>B</b> (a little smaller and cooler) circle each other once every <b>79.76 years</b> on a stretched orbit: <b>11.2 au</b> apart at their closest, <b>35.4 au</b> at their farthest (Akeson et al. 2021, from the radio telescope ALMA and thousands of speed measurements). Their masses are 1.0788 and 0.9092 Suns, and the pair is <b>4.344 light years</b> from us. Two older orbits (Pourbaix &amp; Boffin 2016, Kervella et al. 2016) give masses about 5 % higher: the published orbits disagree by more than their error bars.</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Proxima Centauri</b>, a small red dwarf (0.12 Suns), is the nearest star to the Sun, 4.2465 light years away (the Gaia satellite). It circles A and B about 12,000 au out, one turn in about <b>half a million years</b>, and its speed shows it is bound to them (8.3 sigma, Akeson et al. 2021). Its orbit’s size and shape are reasonably firm; its tilt was revised by tens of degrees between 2017 and 2021. Proxima flares: in March 2016 it became about 68 times brighter, bright enough to see without a telescope, and at least five such superflares happen a year (Howard et al. 2018).</p>"},
    {"history", "🔭 Names and history",
     "<p class=\"lrnP\">Thomas Henderson published a parallax of Alpha Centauri in 1839, from observations made in 1832–33 at the Cape of Good Hope, just after Bessel’s 61 Cygni (as Perryman 2012 tells it). Proxima was discovered by Robert Innes in 1915, and its link with Alpha Centauri was noticed at once (as Kervella et al. 2017 tell it).</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\">The star catalogue the lab uses (HYG v4.1) names A <b>Rigil Kentaurus</b>, B <b>Toliman</b> and the third star <b>Proxima Centauri</b>.</p>"},
    {"planets", "🪐 The planets: confirmed, disputed and refuted",
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Proxima b: confirmed.</b> Found in 2016 by the wobble it gives its star (Anglada-Escudé et al.), seen again by the ESPRESSO and NIRPS spectrographs: at least 1.055 Earth masses, one orbit every 11.18 days, 0.048 au from Proxima, inside its habitable zone, getting about 64 % of the starlight Earth gets (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2025).</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Proxima d: confirmed by NIRPS in 2025.</b> At least 0.26 Earth masses, 5.12 days, 0.029 au; seen first in 2020 and called a candidate in 2022 (Faria et al.).</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Proxima c: disputed.</b> Proposed in 2020 (Damasso et al.): about 6 Earth masses or more, 1.5 au, 1,900 days. A new way of measuring the star’s speeds argues against it: the signal looks like a small error of the method the older speeds were measured with (Artigau et al. 2022); and NIRPS finds no significant signal (2025). The lab draws it dashed.</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>A candidate around A: unconfirmed, seen once.</b> The James Webb Space Telescope saw a faint point next to A in August 2024, perhaps the same as a point seen from the ground in 2019 (Wagner et al. 2021); it was not seen in February and April 2025. If it is real it is a giant planet of about 90–150 Earth masses (Beichman et al. 2025). The lab draws its possible orbits as a faint dashed band.</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Refuted:</b> a planet of Alpha Centauri B announced in 2012 (“Bb”): the signal was a ghost of when the measurements were taken (Rajpaul et al. 2016), and Hubble saw no transit of it (Demory et al. 2015). A dust belt round Proxima reported from ALMA in 2017 (Anglada et al.): a flare explains the extra light, with “no need to invoke the presence of a dust belt” (MacGregor et al. 2018).</p>"},
    {"unknown", "❓ What nobody knows, and what the picture assumes",
     "<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>tilt</b> of the planets’ orbits is not measured, so only their <b>minimum masses</b> are known. The lab draws them in Proxima’s equator plane (tilted 47°, Klein et al. 2021): an <b>assumption</b>, as is the direction of their node. In that plane b would be 1.44 and d 0.36 Earth masses.</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\">Their <b>sizes</b> are not known (they do not pass in front of their star as seen from Earth): the dots are not to scale. Nor is it known whether they have air or water.</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\">The stars’ <b>age</b> is disputed: 5.3 ± 0.3 billion years (Joyce &amp; Chaboyer 2018) or 7.2–7.8 (Thévenin et al. 2026), by the method.</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\">The scene shows the system <b>now</b>: the light we see from it left 4.3 years ago, and light needs up to 0.19 years to cross from Proxima to A and B. Neither delay is drawn.</p>"},
    {"plans", "🚀 Plans to look closer and to go there",
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>TOLIMAN</b> is a small space telescope (12.5 cm across, on a 16U CubeSat) that will measure the positions of A and B against each other so precisely that the pull of a rocky planet in a temperate orbit round either star would show; it is under construction (Tuthill et al. 2026).</p>"
     "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Breakthrough Starshot</b> studies sending tiny probes on light sails pushed by lasers on the ground. Its design for a trip to Alpha Centauri at 0.2 of the speed of light uses a sail 4.1 m across, and the ground beam director would cost about $8.0 billion (Parkin 2018).</p>"},
};
static const int kAcenSectionCount = sizeof(kAcen) / sizeof(kAcen[0]);
